A||||3||Carrolles||New Heaslamp Bulbs||||14||
Z||000000||Carrolles||02-07-2000||01:00 PM||||I just installed the high output headlights that I got from Walmart.  They are the Sylvania Halogen (I forget the suffix) that come boxed in a set of two for around $16.00.  Boy are they nice.<P>I always thought the headlights on my 85SE left something to be desired.  These new bulbs are great.<P>I had to pull out the "Do-It Yourself" manual to figure how to get the two bottom screws out.  I couldn't find anything in the Helms manual.<P>This is about the most involved procedure for changing headlamps I've ever encountered.<P>Anyway the new bulbs are very much worth the trouble.||||reg||14
Z||000001||DaveL||02-09-2000||03:23 PM||||So, there are Female Fiero lovers after all! You are absolutely right, they are the most difficult headlights on the planet to replace. I'm about to do mine, but the screws are all rusted and I'm afraid of what will happen. Hope my JB Blaster works!<P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||02-09-2000||05:18 PM||||Yes there are.  However I am not one of them.  My wife, who is also named Carol, does enjoy the car though.<P>Theogre (or maybe it was Cliff) has a simple procedure where he pops the headlight motor linkage loose at the ball socket and tilts the whole mechanism back thus exposing the bezel screws.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-09-2000||06:38 PM||||Actually I disconnect the linkage on the bottom of the lift. I don't mess with the motor. you need a T30 Torx bit and, I think, a 10mm wrench. You can change a lamp in about 5-10 minutes and you can deal with any striped screws quickly.<P>Also you don't have to fool with the bucket spring or the alignment screws.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-09-2000).]||||reg||1
